# Client material never lands in the engine repo. The engine is
# clean-room: a client is a config file and an index directory, both of which
# live outside this tree in the client's own private repo.
configs/*.toml
#
# ⚠️ **ONE NEGATION PER SHIPPED FILE, and adding a file means adding a line.**
#
# `!configs/example.toml` alone was the whole exception list, so
# `configs/example_evals.toml` — the eval-suite template — had never been
# committed. A fresh clone had no eval example and
# `test_the_example_suite_is_valid` failed there while passing in the working
# tree, which is the same ignore-pattern failure happening twice in one repo.
#
# Found by the clean-clone check that rule requires. It is NOT written
# as `!configs/example*.toml`: a glob would silently re-include a client's file
# that happened to be named `example-acme.toml`, and that is the direction of
# this rule that must never fail.
!configs/example.toml
!configs/example_evals.toml
#
# ⚠️ **ANCHORED TO THE ROOT, and the leading slash is load-bearing.**
#
# Written as `index/`, this matched **`src/stillroom/index/`** as well as the
# client's index directory — so `embeddings.py`, `retrieval.py` and `store.py`
# were **never committed**, and a fresh clone of this repo could not import the
# package at all:
#
#     ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'stillroom.index'
#
# Nothing local ever noticed, because Docker builds from the working tree and
# the tests run against it: every artifact anybody looked at was assembled from
# files git did not have. Found 2026-07-22 by cloning the repo, which is the
# only way this is visible. Same reasoning as `corpus/`.
/corpus/
/index/
*.client.toml

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.env
